# R42 revision 9 — check 16, first leg: what verifies a prebuilt artifact before it
# reaches a contributor's PATH? Revision 7 substituted `cargo binstall` for
# `cargo install --locked` on the owner's licence ruling and proved (check 15) that
# `--locked` binds only the compile fallback. This leg establishes what, if anything,
# binds the prebuilt path instead.
#
# WHY THIS LEG WAS REWRITTEN FOR REVISION 9. Revision 8's payload PRINTED the status of
# every critical command and the two `Verified signature` counts and asserted none of
# them, so a wrong status or a wrong count still left the script exiting zero. That was
# the round-8 rejection (finding F1) and it was correct. Every claimed control is now an
# ASSERTION: it prints `ASSERT-OK <name>` or `ASSERT-FAIL <name>`, a failure increments a
# counter, and a non-zero counter makes the script exit 1. The script also asserts its own
# assertion COUNT (45), so an assertion that is silently skipped fails the run. `set -e` is
# deliberately not used — the 7d negative control expects status 76 — and no assertion is
# evaluated inside `$(...)`, where a subshell would swallow it.
#
# THE CONTROLS ARE DEMONSTRATED, NOT ASSERTED. `PERTURB=<name>` substitutes exactly ONE
# input; the third leg (`evidence/binstall-assertion-controls.log`) runs this script once
# per control with that substitution and requires the run to exit non-zero AND to print
# that control's own `ASSERT-FAIL` line. The seven controls of this leg — the tag-pinned
# `args.rs` hash, the published signing verdict, 7a's zero-count, 7b's fall-through text,
# 7c's one-count, 7d's status 76 and the unchanged `~/.cargo/bin` listing — were each shown
# failing that way. An unperturbed run, which is what this log records, must pass.
#
# Runs: 2026-09-14T23:01:46Z and 23:01:54Z, which is 2026-09-14 16:01 America/Los_Angeles
# (the stamps are the mtimes of the two outputs; the script prints no timestamp of its own).
# The execution recorded below is the FIRST; the second establishes the rerun rule. The
# third leg ran this script eight more times in the same session — the seven perturbed runs
# of its section D and a third clean run after them — and its own log records those.
#
# Working directory: the run directory. Scratch directory: <scratch>/r42r9/ctl/leg1, outside
# the repository. This script reads no file inside the repository and writes none, so
# committing this revision cannot change what it does. Nothing was installed: the v1.23.0
# release asset is unzipped into the scratch directory and put on PATH for the duration of
# the script only, every binstall call carries --dry-run, and the listing of ~/.cargo/bin is
# hashed before and after and the two are ASSERTED EQUAL. That listing hashes to
# 01a2cddcc8ceeb3c17d93fc7bec5d3cbf440ee7416dd81374e4b982acfa389ed, the same value checks 15
# and 16 recorded, so this host's tool set is unchanged since those checks. Its User-Agent
# names the repository by URL and carries no personal contact address.
#
# RERUN RULE, stated for both cases.
# (a) On THIS host: byte-for-byte, except three lines when the rerun uses the SAME scratch
#     directory — the three `INFO Done in <elapsed>` wall-clock timings in steps 7a, 7b and
#     7c. That is what the two runs above differed in, and section A of
#     `evidence/binstall-assertion-controls.log` prints that diff in full and fails if any
#     differing line is not a timing. A rerun in a DIFFERENT scratch directory adds a fourth
#     differing line, `command -v cargo-binstall -> …`, carrying that directory's path.
# (b) On ANY OTHER host: what must match are all 45 assertions and the final line
#     `CHECK16 RESULT: PASSED` — every exit status (0 throughout, and 76 for the negative
#     control), every sha256 of a file fetched at the immutable tag v1.23.0, every source
#     excerpt quoted from those files, the three PRESENT/ABSENT signing verdicts in step 6
#     (crates.io versions are immutable), the count `7a 'Verified signature' occurrences: 0`,
#     the count `7c 'Verified signature' occurrences: 1`, and the fall-through to
#     `cargo install cargo-shear --version 1.13.1 --locked` under --only-signed in step 7b.
#     These lines carry host or target state and WILL differ:
#       1. the scratch path, $CARGO_HOME and the resolved `cargo` executable inside the
#          `Dry-run: running …` and `- <bin> => …` lines.
#       2. the two `~/.cargo/bin` listing sha256 lines — this host's installed tool set.
#          The script asserts they equal EACH OTHER; their value is meaningless across hosts.
#       3. the asset name `cargo-binstall-aarch64-apple-darwin.zip` and the downloaded
#          binary's sha256 `5e17743f…` — Darwin/arm64 only, and neither is asserted for that
#          reason. A Linux rerun must edit the asset name to the matching artifact.
#       4. the target triple inside each WARN line. Note step 7c resolved
#          `(x86_64-apple-darwin)` on this arm64 host, because cargo-binstall's own release
#          maps that target to a `zip` artifact; the signature verification is unaffected and
#          the `Verified signature` line precedes it.
#       5. the `trusted comment` fields inside 7c's `Verified signature` line — `gh=`, `git=`,
#          `ts=` and `run=` are the values cargo-bins' release workflow signed into the
#          artifact for v1.23.0, so they are fixed for that version but would move for
#          another. The assertion is keyed on `Verified signature for package
#          'cargo-binstall'` and on the count, not on those fields.
#
# WHAT THIS CHECK DOES NOT SHOW. No artifact was installed, so no post-install verification
# was observed. Nothing here measures how many of the tools the template will actually
# declare publish a signing key; the three specs below are the ones check 15 already
# exercised plus cargo-binstall itself. Nothing here was timed.
#
# ---8<--- check16.sh ---8<---
